{
  // The Cloudflare Worker `opennextjs-cloudflare build` produces.
  //
  // This is how the application ships. Not a second target, not a spare: the
  // Workers Builds Git integration connected to this repository is the only
  // thing that deploys it.
  //
  //   push to `main`      → `npx wrangler deploy`          → production
  //   push to any branch  → `npx wrangler versions upload` → a preview version
  //
  // Those two commands, and the production branch they key off, are set in the
  // Cloudflare dashboard — not here, and not anywhere in this repository. So a
  // question about which branch goes live is never answered by reading this
  // file. It is answered under Workers & Pages → `ops-talaliving` → Settings →
  // Builds.
  //
  // (There is a Vercel project in this organisation. It belongs to the *old*
  // repository and serves previews there. It has nothing to do with this one,
  // whatever older comments in this repository may say.)
  //
  // Note what is NOT here: no `routes`, no custom domain. Until one is added,
  // a production deploy is reachable only on its own `workers.dev` URL, and
  // pointing `ops.talaliving.com` at this Worker is a deliberate, separate
  // step — a DNS cutover, not a side effect of merging.
  //
  // Deliberately small. There is no R2 incremental cache, no self-reference
  // service binding and no Images binding, because this application has
  // nothing that would use them: every screen is a client component reading
  // browser-held fixtures or Supabase from the browser, there are no route
  // handlers, no middleware, no `next/image`, and no ISR to cache. A binding
  // pointing at a bucket nobody created is a deploy that fails at the worst
  // moment; add them here the day a server route actually needs one. That day
  // is more likely now that this is the production host, not less.
  // ── Where the Supabase keys go, and why not here ────────────────────────
  //
  // Three variables decide whether this deployment talks to a database at all:
  //
  //   NEXT_PUBLIC_USE_SUPABASE=1     somebody choosing
  //   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL       \ something to choose
  //   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY  /
  //
  // `isRealApi()` requires all three. Set only the two keys and the app stays
  // in demo mode with no error and no warning — deliberate (demo is a mode, not
  // a fallback), and a genuine trap: it looks configured and is not.
  //
  // **They belong in Settings → Builds → Build variables, NOT in Settings →
  // Variables and Secrets.** `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is not a naming convention: it is
  // an instruction to Next.js to *inline the value into the client bundle at
  // build time*. By the time the Worker runs, `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_…` in
  // browser code is already a literal. A runtime variable arrives far too late
  // and changes nothing, however correct it looks in the dashboard.
  //
  // Two consequences worth stating once. Setting a build variable does not
  // rebuild anything, so a deploy has to follow — Retry deployment, or a push
  // to `main`. And they are not `vars` in this file, because a value committed
  // here is a value in the repository (A15), and because the anon key differs
  // between a local stack and the project.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "ops-talaliving",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",

  // `nodejs_compat` is required by the adapter — Next's server runtime reaches
  // for node builtins regardless of how little of it this app uses.
  // `global_fetch_strictly_public` makes `fetch()` inside the worker go out to
  // the public internet rather than looping back into this same worker, which
  // is what Next assumes when it fetches its own routes.
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-17",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat", "global_fetch_strictly_public"],

  // The static half of the build — everything under `.open-next/assets` is
  // served from Cloudflare's edge without waking the worker at all.
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },

  // ── Runtime variables ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // **Declared here because a deploy would otherwise remove them.** Plain-text
  // variables set in the dashboard and not present in this file do not survive
  // `wrangler deploy`, which is what every push to `main` runs. Cloudflare says
  // so in the dashboard — *update your Wrangler config to keep deployments in
  // sync* — and the failure it prevents is quiet: the secret below stays, this
  // value vanishes, `driveConfigured()` turns false, and every upload starts
  // answering *the Drive service account is not set*.
  //
  // This is not the contradiction of the note above about `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`. Those
  // are build-time values inlined into the browser bundle, and an anon key that
  // differs between a local stack and the project. This is a **service account
  // identity** — not a secret, the same in every environment, and already
  // written down in `docs/plan/phase-2/05-storage.md`.
  //
  // `GOOGLE_PRIVATE_KEY` is **not** here and never will be. It is a Secret,
  // set once in the dashboard or with `wrangler secret put`, encrypted at rest
  // and unreadable afterwards. Secrets are managed apart from `vars` precisely
  // so that a deploy cannot touch them.
  // `GOOGLE_CHAT_AUDIENCE` belongs in this block too, the day the Chat app
  // exists. It is the Chat app's Cloud **project number** — not a secret, the
  // same in every environment, and the `aud` claim the verifier checks
  // (`src/lib/chat/verify.ts`). Setting it only in the dashboard is the trap
  // this whole block exists to warn about: the next `wrangler deploy` removes
  // it, `/api/chat/events` starts answering 503, and Chat's retries stop.
  //
  // It is absent rather than blank on purpose. An empty string refuses every
  // token, which is the safe failure — a verifier that accepts anything when
  // its audience is unset would be switched off by a typo.
  "vars": {
    "GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL": "capture-worker@john-lau-v01.iam.gserviceaccount.com"
  }
}
